==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*nemaną}}, probably from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*nem-||take}}.

Germanic cognates include Old High German {{term|neman|lang=goh}} (German {{term|nehmen|lang=de}}), Old Saxon {{term|niman|lang=osx}} (Dutch {{term|nemen|lang=nl}}), Old Norse {{term|nema|lang=non}} (Danish {{term|nemme|lang=da}}), Gothic {{term|𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽|tr=niman|lang=got|sc=Goth}}.  The Indo-European root was also the source of Old Irish {{term|nem||present|lang=sga}}, Latvian {{term|ņemt|lang=lv}} ("to take"), Albanian {{term|nëm||to take, curse|lang=sq}}, Ancient Greek {{term|νέμω|tr=nemō|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}}.

